ALFADetect — In-Depth SQA Audit & Google AI Studio Fix Brief
Reviewed: 21 October 2026
Scope: Uploaded React + TypeScript + Vite frontend, Express server.ts/serverDb.ts, and the uploaded ALFADetect SRS (with speaker verification).
Review type: Static source-code and requirements review. I could not claim a successful runtime/browser test: this ZIP has no node_modules, and the app was not launched in a browser during this review. Treat runtime, accessibility, and responsive findings as needing verification after fixes.
Executive SQA verdict
Current status: NOT READY for a professional forensic demo or real case use. The project has a substantial, visually styled multi-screen shell, but important screens present hardcoded fictional findings, fake audit/custody data, synthetic progress, and local-only actions as if they were real forensic operations. Several central workflows do not call the existing backend. This directly conflicts with the request for a real frontend with no dummy/fake data.

Target approach: Keep the existing green/gold visual identity if desired, but remove fabricated operational data. The UI must be attractive because of layout, typography, spacing, clear states, real audio-derived visuals when available, and excellent empty/loading/error states—not because it displays invented 98.4% scores, fake cases, fake hashes, or pretend signatures. AI-model work is in progress, so show “Model not connected / Analysis unavailable” rather than inventing results.
Severity guide
- P0 — Critical: Misrepresents evidence, integrity, identity, analysis, certification, or legal conclusions.
- P1 — High: Core workflow does not persist, connect to backend, or meet an SRS requirement.
- P2 — Medium: Usability, accessibility, reliability, security-hardening, or incomplete interaction.
- P3 — Polish: Visual consistency and refinements.
1. Critical findings (P0)
P0-01 — Detection Results are hardcoded, not tied to uploaded audio
Location: src/components/screens/DetectionResultsScreen.tsx
The screen defines a fixed array of six suspicious anomalies with fixed time ranges, frequency bands, severities, confidence scores, and explanations. The audio playback button uses text-to-speech to narrate a finding instead of playing the actual audio segment. The values are not fetched from /api/analyze-audio or derived from an analysis result.

Risk: A user can upload any file and still see a pre-written deepfake conclusion and made-up timestamps. This is unacceptable for forensic evidence and violates FR-07, FR-08, NFR-08, NFR-24, NFR-25 and the SRS principle that conclusions are probabilistic decision support.

Required fix: Results page must render only the analysis record returned by the backend for the selected evidence ID. If there is no completed analysis, show a polished empty state: “No analysis available for this evidence” plus a “Run analysis” action when authorized. Do not show scores, anomalies, timestamps, model names, or conclusions unless the API returned them. Audio segment controls must seek/play the actual uploaded audio, and be disabled with a clear explanation if segment data/audio is unavailable.
P0-02 — AI pipeline simulates completion and displays an invented verdict
Location: src/components/screens/AiPipelineScreen.tsx
runFullPipeline() advances through ten stages on a timer, irrespective of any server/model result. Initial state is set to stage 10. The page always displays “DEEPFAKE (SYNTHETIC), 97.3% (±0.4%), VITS / HiFi-GAN Vocoder”. These values are fabricated and the pipeline does not call /api/analyze-audio.

Risk: False representation that a model ran and that evidence is synthetic. Violates FR-07/08, NFR-02, NFR-08, NFR-21, NFR-25, NFR-26 and FR-57.

Required fix: Make the action call the backend using the selected case/evidence ID and show server-derived queued/running/succeeded/partial-failure/failed states. Do not fake step completion. Show each model’s real status, inference duration, result, confidence/calibration metadata, and error. While the AI API is not connected, disable the action or show “AI model service not configured”; do not display a fake verdict. Start in an explicit idle state, not 10/10 complete.
P0-03 — Hash screen fabricates successful integrity verification
Location: src/components/screens/HashIntegrityScreen.tsx
Missing evidence hashes are replaced with a hardcoded 64-character-looking value. verificationResult initializes to MATCH. Recompute sets the current hash equal to the original hash without re-reading the file. “Test Tamper Detection” inserts a fixed fake hash and uses timers to simulate a result. It does not verify the selected evidence against the server-stored original bytes/hash.

Risk: UI can claim evidence is intact when there is no trusted baseline or no file loaded. Violates FR-04, FR-13, FR-36, NFR-06, NFR-28.

Required fix: Default to UNKNOWN / NOT VERIFIED, never MATCH. No hardcoded fallback hash. Recompute SHA-256 from actual file bytes and compare against the immutable server-stored ingestion hash through a real endpoint. If original hash/file is missing, return “Cannot verify — original baseline unavailable”. Separate any test-only tampering demo into an explicitly labelled developer/test fixture and never let it write into real cases or audit logs.
P0-04 — Report generation downloads plain text with misleading file extensions
Location: src/components/screens/ReportGenerationScreen.tsx
handleDownload() creates a Blob with type: 'text/plain' but names it .pdf, .docx, or .html depending on the selected option. The report body contains fixed sample conclusions (98.4% confidence, fixed model metrics, fixed timestamps), fictional analysts/expert witnesses, a fake biometric verification percentage, and a fabricated digital signature. The date is hardcoded to 2026-08-10. biometricVerified is hardcoded true. Hash falls back to a fabricated value.

Risk: A text file is masqueraded as PDF/DOCX; report contains invented forensic evidence and credentials. Extremely serious for court-facing software. Violates FR-15/18/53, NFR-25, and acceptance criteria 5–6.

Required fix: Use the backend’s actual report-generation endpoint if available; otherwise build a genuine PDF only when real result records exist. Do not offer DOCX/HTML unless each is correctly generated. Report fields must come from case, evidence, analysis, speaker-verification, condition/disguise, expert-review and custody API data. Use the current server timestamp. Never claim digital signing, biometric verification, certification, expert approval, or “court-ready” status unless a real verified operation supports it. Preliminary reports must be clearly labelled preliminary and must list unavailable analyses. If there is no real result, block final report generation and explain why.
P0-05 — Chain-of-custody page shows fabricated case history
Location: src/components/screens/ChainOfCustodyScreen.tsx
The page declares hardcoded events, fictional officers, timestamps, IP addresses, hashes, digital signatures, and statuses. It imports MOCK_CHAIN_OF_CUSTODY but defines its own fixed chain as well. It does not load the selected case’s /api/cases/:caseId/chain-of-custody endpoint.

Risk: Presents a fake audit trail as evidence provenance. Violates FR-13, FR-15, FR-22, FR-54, NFR-15, NFR-28.

Required fix: Fetch chain events for the selected case from the backend; display no event until the server returns one. Empty state must say “No custody events recorded”. Every event must show server-provided actor, UTC timestamp, action, evidence ID/hash, and verification status. Never invent IP addresses, signatures, or block hashes. A chain of custody should not be described as immutable or digitally signed unless the backend actually enforces/verifies it.
P0-06 — XAI visuals/SHAP/spectrogram include fabricated artifacts
Location: src/components/screens/ExplainableAiScreen.tsx; src/utils/pitchExtractor.ts
The XAI page defines fixed suspicious regions and imports MOCK_SHAP_FEATURES. The fallback creates synthetic pitch data if there is no evidence audio or decoding fails. Code comments explicitly describe simulated spectrogram frequency bins/artificial cutoffs. This can make visualizations look like they came from an analysis when they did not.

Risk: Explanations do not explain the selected model’s actual output. Violates FR-09, FR-10, FR-34, FR-49 and NFR-11/NFR-24/NFR-25.

Required fix: XAI screen must use backend-generated Grad-CAM/SHAP/attention/segment artifacts, tied to the exact analysis ID, model version and evidence hash. If XAI output is not available, show a designed placeholder saying “Explanation unavailable for this run”; do not draw fake anomaly heatmaps. Client-side F0 extraction can be a separate genuine descriptive signal-processing tool, but must label it as DSP-only, must not be called model explainability, and must not silently replace failures with synthetic speech data.
P0-07 — Evidence preview/playback speaks a fabricated narration instead of the audio
Location: src/components/screens/EvidenceManagementScreen.tsx; src/components/screens/ReportGenerationScreen.tsx; src/components/screens/DetectionResultsScreen.tsx
Several play buttons use speakAudioEvidence() with generated narration. The evidence upload page creates local object URLs and updates local state but does not call /api/evidence/upload. It assigns a hardcoded case ID, duration, sample rate, bit depth, channel count, and status. Recording is also handled locally and then labelled as a WAV even though MediaRecorder commonly emits a browser-specific WebM/Opus format unless encoding is actually converted.

Risk: The selected “evidence” may not exist on the backend, metadata is wrong, and playback can be mistaken for original evidence. Violates FR-03/04/05/12, FR-22/24, NFR-09.

Required fix: Upload the actual file to the backend and only show it as ingested after server confirmation and server hash return. Read actual metadata from a real parser/backend; if a property is unavailable, show “Unknown”, not a guessed value. The primary play button must play the uploaded original audio. Any narration/demo must be a separate, clearly labelled accessibility/demo feature. Validate WAV/MP3/FLAC, max 50 MB, actual content type, duration and speech quality. For microphone recording, preserve the real MIME type or transcode explicitly before calling it WAV. Release object URLs and microphone tracks on unmount/stop.
P0-08 — Landing page advertises fictional demo samples/credentials
Location: src/components/screens/LandingScreen.tsx
Landing page includes hardcoded “DEMO_SAMPLES”, fabricated waveform/spectrogram animation, sample model conclusions, and text that refers to simulated law-enforcement/defence credentials. A demo can be useful, but it must never look like real case data or suggest production credentials.

Required fix: Either remove demo results or isolate them in a “Sandbox demonstration — synthetic sample data, not real analysis” area with a visible banner on every demo screen. Never seed production cases/users. No default/demo passwords or fabricated access credentials should be advertised as real.
P0-09 — Public report verification simulates intermediate checks
Location: src/components/screens/PublicVerificationScreen.tsx
The screen shows “Token structure valid” and “Record located in ledger” before the backend response is received. These are timer-based simulated checks. The backend is called only later.

Risk: A user can infer the token was validated before the real verification result. “Ledger” is also misleading unless a ledger exists.

Required fix: Only show a neutral “Contacting verification service…” while waiting. Every step must be driven by actual server verification output. Show verified/unverified/expired/revoked/unavailable based on the response; do not imply blockchain/ledger validation unless implemented.
P0-10 — Expert certification and biometric flow contains simulated biometric/certification elements
Location: src/components/screens/ExpertValidationScreen.tsx; src/components/screens/ReportGenerationScreen.tsx
ExpertValidation contains a simulated thumb-camera canvas, random data, fake certificate ID fallback, and direct mutation of MOCK_AUDIT_LOGS. Report screen claims biometric verification is true by default. There are backend endpoints for biometrics/certification, but UI must not turn a simulation into a claim of real identity verification.

Required fix: Remove simulated biometric confirmation from the production workflow. If biometric integration is not available, mark “Not configured” and do not certify identity. Certification IDs must be returned by the backend. Expert approve/dispute/needs-review actions must call the server, include a reason where required, and refresh persisted review/audit data.
2. High-priority functional and integration issues (P1)
P1-01 — Case creation is local-only
Location: src/components/screens/CaseManagementScreen.tsx
handleCreateCase() creates a random ID and only calls React setCases. It does not POST /api/cases. It also sets evidenceCount: 1 even though no evidence has been uploaded.

Required fix: POST the case form to /api/cases, use the server-returned case ID/record, show validation and API errors, set evidence count to zero until evidence is attached, then refresh case list. Add edit/close/update actions that use server endpoints. Do not use Math.random() for case IDs.
P1-02 — Evidence management does not use backend upload/list APIs
Location: src/components/screens/EvidenceManagementScreen.tsx
Local file selection creates a browser object URL and sets local evidence state only. No use of /api/evidence/upload or /api/evidence. “Verified” is set without server verification. MOCK_EVIDENCE is imported.

Required fix: Integrate upload/list/detail endpoints. Handle progress, duplicate upload, network failure, 50 MB cap, supported format, server hash, upload confirmation, and retry. Evidence list must be scoped to selected case. Prevent analysis until server confirms integrity.
P1-03 — Speaker verification and reference voice workflows are absent from the UI
SRS: FR-21–36, FR-54, UC-12, UC-13
Backend endpoints present in this project: GET/POST /api/cases/:caseId/references, GET /api/cases/:caseId/speaker-verifications, POST /api/speaker-verification/compare.

Finding: No frontend screen was found that calls these routes. Existing Evidence Management is not a substitute for a dedicated reference voice note workflow.

Required fix: Add a Reference Voice Samples panel within each case: subject, reference file, condition tag, genuine-sample label, consent/lawful-authority declaration, hash, duration/quality validation, upload status, multiple references per subject, and removal/deletion only where policy permits. Add “Verify Speaker” workflow that selects one evidence voice and one/more references, requires hash integrity pass, calls compare endpoint, and shows Match / No Match / Inconclusive, similarity, calibrated probability if returned, thresholds, speech duration, model agreement, segment timeline, diarization, limitations, and errors. Never invent results.
P1-04 — Voice condition/disguise analysis not surfaced as a real workflow
SRS: FR-37–49; backend route POST /api/voice-analysis/condition, GET case voice-disguise route.
Finding: No UI call to these APIs was found. The current hardcoded pitch/anomaly cards are not a substitute for illness/disguise classification.

Required fix: Add separate result cards for natural voice, illness indicators, human disguise, software-altered voice, AI-generated voice; display class probabilities only if returned by the model service. Include pitch contour, spectrogram/formant comparison, replay-attack/mimicry flags and confidence/limitations only when actual data exists. Keep deepfake detection distinct from speaker identity and voice disguise. Apply SRS combined-verdict decision matrix, including no unconditional speaker Match for AI-generated audio.
P1-05 — Model comparison is entirely mock-data driven
Location: src/components/screens/ModelComparisonScreen.tsx
Uses MOCK_MODEL_RESULTS and RADAR_COMPARISON_DATA, with hardcoded performance charts. These can be mistaken for measured model performance.

Required fix: Fetch actual deployed model registry and measured evaluation metrics from an admin/model API. If no endpoint exists, show “No model metrics connected” and document the backend contract needed. Never invent accuracy/precision/recall/F1/speed or imply a model is deployed.
P1-06 — Dashboard mixes real endpoints with non-persisted frontend state
Location: src/components/screens/DashboardScreen.tsx; src/App.tsx
Dashboard calls /api/dashboard/stats and /api/audit-logs, while case creation and evidence uploads remain local. This can create contradictory counts and records. Audit fetching uses plain fetch in places instead of the shared auth client.

Required fix: Use one typed API client for authenticated requests, consistent error handling, and backend source of truth. Refresh dashboard/cases/evidence after mutations. Avoid displaying stale counts as live values.
P1-07 — Admin user list is seeded from mock users
Location: src/components/screens/AdminPanelScreen.tsx
internalUsers initializes from MOCK_USERS, while App separately loads real /api/users. This creates two competing user stores and can show fictional users or edits that are not persisted.

Required fix: Use users prop/backend response only. Every role/status change must call the corresponding API and update only after success. Backend must enforce permissions; frontend role hiding is not security.
P1-08 — Settings reports successful saves without persistence
Location: src/components/screens/SettingsScreen.tsx
Account, 2FA, notification preferences, appearance and retention handlers update local component state or show success banners, but do not persist to server. The account save changes App state but does not PATCH the profile endpoint. Several defaults are invented, e.g. phone, enabled 2FA, YubiKey name and retention periods.

Required fix: Wire each control to a documented endpoint or label it “UI preference only / not connected”. Do not display “saved”, “committed”, or “applied” unless persistence succeeds. Do not prefill invented personal/security data. Add loading/error/success states and persist preferences intentionally.
P1-09 — Audit log exports and expert/report actions mutate frontend mock arrays
Location: src/components/screens/AuditLogScreen.tsx, ExpertValidationScreen.tsx, ReportGenerationScreen.tsx
Some actions alter MOCK_AUDIT_LOGS in memory. Such events disappear on refresh and do not reach the server’s audit log.

Required fix: All meaningful actions must be audited server-side. UI should only read the returned audit records and never mutate a client-side audit array as the source of truth. Audit export must export real filtered records and report the actual exported count.
P1-10 — Public and legal/report states need real authorization and provenance
SRS: FR-14/15/18/52/53 and NFR-07/23/25
Ensure only authorized roles can see sensitive evidence, reference voiceprints, model details and full reports. Public verification should reveal only fields explicitly approved for public disclosure. A public token must not expose private evidence/audio, voice embeddings, investigator details, internal audit IPs or case notes.
P1-11 — Auth/session flow needs resilience and consistent API usage
Location: src/App.tsx, src/utils/api.ts, SignInScreen.tsx, RequestAccessScreen.tsx
App initializes authenticated state from presence of a localStorage token before server validation, then validates asynchronously. On a network failure, the catch logs a warning but does not reliably clear the initial authenticated UI. Plain fetch and authFetch are mixed. The client should handle 401/403 consistently, avoid sending token to public routes, and show a session-expired/error state.

Required fix: Treat session as “checking” until /api/auth/me returns success. If server cannot be reached, show an offline/service error and do not assume authenticated. Clear invalid tokens on 401, centralize API handling, and do not store secrets in client-side logs. Confirm server uses secure cookie or an explicitly justified token strategy; localStorage tokens are exposed to successful XSS.
P1-12 — Case/evidence selection is not consistently propagated
Location: src/App.tsx and screens
Several pages use hardcoded fallback IDs such as DS-2024-4471 when no case is selected. Evidence upload assigns that same case ID. This can display unrelated data and make actions run against the wrong case.

Required fix: Remove fallback IDs and use an explicit “Select a case/evidence first” state. Every API mutation must include the selected case/evidence identifier, and responses must be checked against that selection before rendering.
