Important scope note: Some NFRs (bcrypt, HTTPS/TLS, encryption at rest, immutable storage, concurrency and model accuracy) are backend/deployment/model obligations, not things a frontend alone can prove. Google AI Studio should not “fake” them in UI; it should show real configuration/status only if an API exposes it.
4. Additional workflow and usability findings (P2)
1. Initial state is misleading: AiPipelineScreen starts at stage 10; HashIntegrityScreen starts at MATCH; report screen assumes biometric verified. Start with neutral “not run / unknown / not available” states.
2. Evidence metadata is invented: duration 03:45, sample rate 44.1 kHz, bit depth 24-bit, stereo and case ID are assigned regardless of file. Do not assume these.
3. Incorrect evidence lifecycle: upload marks status “Verified” without server confirmation; case creation sets evidenceCount: 1 before evidence exists.
4. Random IDs: frontend uses Math.random() for case/evidence/certification IDs. IDs must be generated by the backend.
5. Mock fallback in DSP: pitchExtractor.ts generates synthetic PCM when decoding fails. A decoding error should produce an error/unsupported-format state, not synthetic output presented beside real evidence.
6. Audio recording MIME type: a MediaRecorder Blob is labelled audio/wav without an actual WAV encoder/transcoding step. Detect actual mediaRecorder.mimeType or transcode server-side.
7. No visible server upload/progress lifecycle: need upload progress, processing state, retry, cancel where safe, duplicate handling and clear API errors.
8. API error handling inconsistent: multiple screens use raw fetch, some use authFetch; JSON parsing/status checks are not consistent. Centralize request, auth, schema validation and error-to-message mapping.
9. No typed API contracts evident for many screens: add request/response types for cases, evidence, analysis, reference samples, verification, voice condition/disguise, reviews, reports and audit events. Validate untrusted server responses at runtime.
10. State becomes stale: changes in child components often only update local React state; refresh or invalidate the backend records after every successful mutation.
11. Browser history/routing needs testing: navigation uses pushState on every screen action; navigateTo exists but many screens receive setCurrentScreen, so history behavior may diverge. Test direct links, browser back/forward, invalid screen values and login transitions.
12. Mobile layout risk: sidebar has fixed width and sticky full-height layout; many screens use dense tables/charts and tiny text. Test at 320px, 375px, 768px, 1024px and desktop. Provide a real mobile drawer, touch-sized controls and responsive tables/cards.
13. Accessibility: ensure every input has a programmatic label, icon-only buttons have accessible names, focus rings remain visible, dialogs trap/restore focus, status is announced to screen readers, color is not the only signal, and charts have text summaries.
14. Loading/empty/error states: every screen needs all four states (loading, real data, empty, error). Do not silently show old data after a failed request.
15. Confirmation and destructive actions: delete/close case, remove reference, revoke token, change role, and retention/deletion actions require clear confirmation and server response.
16. Clipboard APIs: clipboard calls can reject outside secure contexts or without permission; catch failures and show a proper message.
17. Object URL/resource cleanup: revoke uploaded audio object URLs when replaced/unmounted; stop microphone tracks and timers on stop/unmount.
18. Potential claims/legal language: phrases such as “court-ready”, “self-authenticating”, “FRE 902(13)/(14)”, “immutable”, “RSA-4096”, “FIPS”, and “ledger” must not be asserted unless the real system performs the relevant operation and legal review has approved the wording.
19. Typography/contrast: design uses a polished green/gold palette, but many labels are 10–11px. Keep the palette, increase key body text to a comfortable size, make primary actions prominent, and maintain contrast.
20. Visual density: some screens are highly packed with forensic jargon, gauges and cards. Prioritize a clear evidence/case summary, actual status, primary action and plain-language interpretation; tuck technical detail into expandable panels.
